  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nBy Saturday morning, the surface cold front will clear the area off \nto the southeast, and will be positioned from western Kansas through \neastern Nebraska. This brings increasing cold advection in its wake, \nbringing much cooler highs than in previous days. Temperatures \nSaturday afternoon range from the low 50s across northwest Nebraska \nto the 60s in southwest Nebraska. Modest isentropic ascent in the \n300-305K layer aloft should be enough to promote increasing shower \ncoverage by Saturday afternoon, mainly across southwest into central \nNebraska. This is in response to increasing warm advection \naloft, in advance of deep upper low pressure beginning to slowly\neject east out of the Four Corner region. This precipitation \nthen looks to persist through much of the day Sunday, as the \nparent upper low finally begins to eject into the Plains by \nMonday morning. The system will have a deep tap to Gulf \nmoisture, promoting PWAT values climbing to above the 99th \npercentile climo for late October. This, combined with the slow \nmotion of the upper dynamics, suggests a somewhat long duration \nefficient rainfall event across much of the area. Meager \nelevated instability is also expected to overspread the area \nSunday night into Monday, suggesting some threat for a embedded \nthunderstorms and further enhanced rainfall rates. These showers\nand a few thunderstorms are expected to continue into Monday \nmorning, before the upper low begins to eject overhead and lends\nto lessening precipitation coverage from west to east through \nthe day. Increasing spread exists with respect to surface \nfeatures by Monday, with a split in deterministic guidance with \nrespect to cold frontal passage and the arrival of colder and \ndrier air. Regardless, the consensus remains that there is \nlittle to no concern for mixed p-types, as temperatures aloft \nremain warm enough to support rain through the expected end of \nthe precipitation. \n\nAs for totals, guidance continues to come into better agreement with \nrespect to a swath of significant (0.5-1\"+) accumulations for a \nlarge portion of southwest into central and north central Nebraska. \nIn fact, NBM probabilities continue to creep upwards, with \nprobabilities of >1\" now as high as 60-75% for areas roughly along \nand east of an Imperial to Ainsworth line. This is further supported \nby ensemble means, with the entirety of the 0Z EPS, GEFS, and CMCE \nensemble suite painting QPF means in excess of 1\" in roughly the \nsame corridor. The EFI also continues to trend upwards with respect \nto anomalous QPF, with and EFI of ~0.6-0.7 and an SOT >0 across\nsouthwest Nebraska into the Sandhills. To sum this all up, \nconfidence is continuing to increase in a very beneficial \nrainfall event, with much of southwest into central and north \ncentral Nebraska seeing accumulations of 0.5-1\"+ through Monday.\n\n\nAs the surface cold front finally clears the area by Tuesday, all \nprecipitation is expected to end across the area. Briefly drier \nconditions then prevail into midweek, though guidance suggests a \nquick return to southerly flow across the area by Tuesday afternoon. \nThis will allow for temperatures to begin to moderate somewhat,\nand highs will return back to the lower 60s by Wednesday and \nThursday.",
